    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `_try_repr_or_str`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_try_repr_or_str` function is designed to safely obtain a string representation of an object, either by using the built-in `repr` function or by falling back to a custom format if an exception occurs. It takes a single parameter, `obj`, which is the object for which a string representation is desired. The function attempts to return the result of `repr(obj)`. If a `KeyboardInterrupt` or `SystemExit` exception is raised, it re-raises the exception to allow the program to handle it appropriately. For any other exception, it returns a string in the format \"{type(obj).__name__}('{obj}')\", which includes the type name and the object itself. This function does not rely on any external helper functions or methods; it uses Python's built-in `repr` function and exception handling mechanisms. It handles errors by catching exceptions and providing a fallback string representation, ensuring that the function does not crash even if the `repr` method of the object raises an exception. There are no side effects; the function does not modify any external state or resources.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration b